<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  Entitlements for the macOS bundle, applied when Tauri signs it. Referenced by
  `bundle.macOS.entitlements` in `tauri.conf.json`.

  The same one key the full app carries, for the same reason and with the same
  caveat. Under the hardened runtime — which notarisation requires, and which a
  Developer ID build gets — a process cannot open the microphone unless it says
  so here, whatever `NSMicrophoneUsageDescription` in `Info.plist` says. The
  plist key is what the *user* is asked; this entitlement is what the *kernel*
  permits. With only the plist, everything works under `tauri dev` and in an
  unsigned build, and capture returns silence in the signed bundle.

  `com.apple.security.app-sandbox` is deliberately absent. The full app has
  since turned the sandbox on for its Mac App Store build (its Entitlements.plist
  has the history); this app has not, and that is a decision of its own to take
  rather than one to make as a side effect of a microphone or file change.

  Unlike the full app, this one would not need
  `com.apple.security.files.user-selected.read-write` if it were sandboxed: it
  shows no save or open panel and reads or writes no file the learner picks.
  Add that key alongside the sandbox if that ever changes.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.device.audio-input</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
